# Agent git hygiene

Work in the **primary clone** by default. That keeps the local dev server attached and the files visible in the Cursor IDE.

**One task → one branch → one concern** still applies. Parallel Cursor agents share one git object database; checkout fights and monolithic stashes are how WIP gets lost. That does **not** mean reaching for a worktree by default — each one is a full checkout (`node_modules` aside, still real disk) and one more path the maintainer has to remember exists, find, and manually validate against. **Worktrees are opt-in**: create one only when it is genuinely necessary (see triggers below) **or** the maintainer explicitly asks for one. When in doubt, stay in the primary clone and ask before creating a worktree.

## One task → one branch → one concern

| Do | Don't |
| --- | --- |
| Create or checkout a **topic branch** before editing | Two agents on the same branch |
| **One feature per branch / PR** | Receipts + envelopes + Plaid in one dirty tree |
| **Commit WIP** on the topic branch (`wip: …`) and push | Park agent handoff in `git stash` |
| **Path-scoped stash** only when the maintainer explicitly asks | `git stash` the whole working tree |

**Stash is not agent handoff.** If work must survive a context switch, it belongs on a **named branch** (local or pushed), not `stash@{n}`.

> **Scope:** "Commit WIP" is not a blanket commit permission. It means an agent ending a session or handing off to another agent should commit rather than stash — still subject to the repo's pre-commit gates. It does **not** authorize autonomous commits in ordinary single-agent sessions; follow the maintainer's explicit request as usual.

## Default: primary clone

Stay in the primary workspace unless a fallback trigger below fires:

- Create or checkout the **topic branch** in the primary clone and edit there.
- Leave the existing **dev server** running in that tree.
- Do **not** proactively create a sibling worktree or `move_agent_to_root` into one.

| Agent / task | Workspace |
| --- | --- |
| Feature implementation (tree is idle for this task) | Primary clone on `feat/<topic>` |
| Feature implementation (primary genuinely contended, or maintainer asked for a worktree) | Worktree — see fallback below |
| Real Steel (primary idle for this PR) | Primary clone on the PR branch |
| Read-only explore | Primary clone |

## Fallback: isolate with a worktree

This is a **fallback**, not a default. Only escalate out of the primary clone when **any** of these is true — including mid-task after a clean start — **or the maintainer explicitly asked for a worktree**:

- `git status` shows **dirty files that are not this task's** (another agent's WIP, mixed concerns) that cannot be resolved by simply asking the maintainer.
- Checkout, merge, or rebase would **clobber** someone else's in-progress edits.
- **Two implementing agents** would write the same working tree at the same time (Multitask, overlapping chats / colliding sessions).
- The primary clone is on a **different branch that has real uncommitted work on it** the maintainer still needs (a clean idle branch is not a reason — just check out the target branch in the primary clone instead).

If none of these clearly apply, **stay in the primary clone**. If you are unsure whether a trigger applies, **ask the maintainer** rather than defaulting to a worktree — creating one is easy to do but easy to forget to clean up, and it costs real disk and a second path to keep straight during manual validation.

When a worktree genuinely is warranted, give this writer its **own worktree directory** and **own branch**:

```bash
# From the primary clone (repo root) — worktree + env copy in one step
./scripts/new-worktree.sh feat/<topic>
# or track an existing PR branch:
./scripts/new-worktree.sh feat/voice-dictation-prefill-form
# manual (remember to copy .env.local afterward — see "Worktree env" below):
git fetch origin && git worktree add ../bitbudget-<short-topic> -b feat/<topic> origin/main
```

`<short-topic>` must be **unique on the filesystem**. When multiple agents may work on related areas concurrently, disambiguate with an issue number or PR number (e.g. `../bitbudget-auth-123`) to avoid path collisions.

**After falling back:**

- Call **`move_agent_to_root` once** to the worktree so the chat’s IDE root is that tree. Cursor auto-approves **in-workspace** file edits; edits under a **sibling** worktree while the chat is rooted elsewhere prompt on **every** `Edit`/`Write`.
- Accept one move approval if Smart Mode asks; that is cheaper than per-file approval spam.
- Launch further `Task` passes with that same worktree as cwd.
- Keep the **primary clone** on the branch/dev-server the other work still needs.
- **Remove worktrees** when the task ends: `git worktree remove --force <path> && git worktree prune`.
- Do **not** delete the PR’s remote feature branch unless the maintainer asks.

## Worktree env (`.env.local`) — copy, never read

A fresh `git worktree add` starts with **no** gitignored local env (`.env.local`, `.env`, `.env.*.local`). The new worktree then runs with missing DB URLs, auth secrets, and encryption-related config — symptoms include 500s on auth/session pages and **failed client crypto**.

- **Always copy local env into a new worktree.** Use the helper, which `git worktree add`s and copies the env files in one step:

```bash
./scripts/new-worktree.sh <branch> [base-branch]   # base defaults to origin/main
```

- **Secrets — copy, never read.** `.env.local` and friends hold live secrets (DB passwords, `AUTH_SECRET`, encryption keys, Plaid/provider tokens). Agents and scripts may **copy** these files (`cp`) but must **never** read, `cat`, `grep`, print, log, or echo their contents, and must never paste env values into chat, commits, or PRs. The script copies with `cp` only for this reason.
- **Restart after copying.** Next.js does not reload `.env.local`; restart the worktree dev server so it picks up the copied env.
- If you create a worktree by hand (`git worktree add` directly), copy the env yourself afterward (`cp ../bitbudget/.env.local <worktree>/`) — do not open the file to "check" it first.

## Cleanup after merge or handoff

After a task is merged, abandoned, or handed back to the maintainer:

1. Confirm no needed WIP remains: `git status` in the worktree (if any) and `git stash list` for topic-scoped stashes.
2. If a fallback worktree was created, remove it from the primary clone: `git worktree remove --force ../bitbudget-<topic> && git worktree prune`. **Agents: do not run `--force` autonomously** — stop and ask the maintainer if step 1 shows any uncommitted work, or if you are uncertain the worktree is fully clean.
3. Delete the local topic branch only after it is merged or intentionally abandoned: `git branch -d feat/<topic>` (use `-D` only with maintainer approval).
4. Delete the remote branch if it was pushed but **never opened as a PR** (no GitHub auto-delete): `git push origin --delete feat/<topic>`. Then prune stale remote-tracking refs: `git fetch --prune origin`.
5. Drop obsolete topic-scoped stashes once their changes are merged, committed elsewhere, or no longer needed.

**Bulk prune (preferred after several merges):** from any clone/worktree of this repo:

```bash
./scripts/git-prune.sh                 # dry-run: gone remotes + safety skips
./scripts/git-prune.sh --worktrees     # also list clean stale worktrees
./scripts/git-prune.sh --apply --worktrees --yes   # delete (maintainer/agent with approval)
```

The script pairs with GitHub **Automatically delete head branches**. It never deletes `main`, the current branch, dirty worktrees, or open-PR heads. Dry-run is the default.

**SessionStart hook (passive maintenance):** `.cursor/hooks.json` runs `.cursor/hooks/git-prune-session-start.sh` on new agent chats (dry-run only; skips background agents). It injects a short debt summary via `additional_context` / session `env`, and writes `.cursor/git-prune-status.md` (local, gitignored). If prune debt is relevant to the task, **Read** that status file or re-run `./scripts/git-prune.sh --worktrees`. Never auto-`--apply` from the hook.

## Before changing branches in the primary clone

1. `git status` — if dirty with **this task's** files, **commit to the topic branch**. If dirty with **someone else's** files, **stop and ask** the maintainer (they may just want you to wait, or may explicitly ask for a worktree); do not auto-stash to “make checkout work,” and do not spin up a worktree on your own initiative unless a fallback trigger clearly applies.
2. If the change belongs to a different concern, **new branch + commit**, not a mixed stash on whatever branch is checked out. Reach for a new worktree only if the primary tree is genuinely contended (see fallback triggers above) or the maintainer asks for one.

## Stash discipline (when stash is unavoidable)

- **Message names the concern and paths:** `stash: envelope sweep — envelope-funding, envelope-card-metrics`
- **Path-scoped:** `git stash push -m "…" -- path/to/file1 path/to/file2`
- Never stash unrelated files (schema, receipts UI, Plaid) in one entry.
- After merging a branch, grep `git stash list` for that topic and drop obsolete entries; stale stashes are not long-term storage.

## Real Steel

Default: run Real Steel in the **primary clone** on the PR branch so the review stays in the IDE tree. Fall back to a dedicated worktree (and `move_agent_to_root` once — see **Fallback** above) only if the primary has other WIP or another agent is writing there, **or** the maintainer asks for a worktree. Do not rebase or commit over unrelated dirty files. The global **real-steel** skill (`~/.cursor/skills/real-steel/SKILL.md`) defines the review/pass workflow; [`.cursor/skills/real-steel/SKILL.md`](../skills/real-steel/SKILL.md) adds BitBudget completion steps.

## Multitask mode

Multitask is fine when each implementing agent has its **own branch**. The first writer uses the **primary clone** if that tree is idle. A second implementing agent that would share the same working directory should **prefer asking the maintainer** (wait/serialize vs worktree); only fall back to a worktree when colliding sessions would clobber the shared tree and waiting is not workable.

**Checklist before starting a second implementing agent:**

- [ ] First agent has its own branch (primary clone OK if the tree was idle).
- [ ] Second agent has a **different** branch; use a **worktree** only for real contention or an explicit maintainer request.
- [ ] No uncommitted mixed WIP on a shared integration branch (e.g. long-lived `feat/foo` with unrelated slices).
